---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PageHeader from "../../components/PageHeader.astro";
import DynamicsEngine from "../../novascope/components/DynamicsEngine.astro";

/*
 * Thin, like /explore/census: the page owns the copy and the framing, the engine
 * owns the interaction. /dynamics-lab is a 762-line monolith, which is exactly
 * why nothing could be reused from it.
 *
 * This is the DYNAMICAL counterpart to /explore/mass-segregation, which shows
 * PRIMORDIAL segregation — a knob set at birth, no clock. Here the same
 * arrangement is earned by integrating gravity. The two link to each other
 * because side by side they are the argument: one assumed, one emergent.
 */
---

<BaseLayout
  title="No star is an island — Anna Rosen"
  description="Turn gravity on and watch a star cluster come alive: the heaviest stars sink, the cluster segregates, and none of it was put in by hand."
  path="/explore/dynamics"
>
  <PageHeader eyebrow="Explore · interactive" title="No star is an island">
    <Fragment slot="lede">
      Until now a cluster has been a list of stars. Switch gravity on and it becomes a
      <em>system</em>: every star pulls on every other, energies get shuffled by close
      passages, and the heaviest stars give up energy and sink toward the centre. Nothing
      below is choreographed — <strong>mass segregation is not a rule that was added</strong>,
      it is what this same gravity does when you let it run.
    </Fragment>
  </PageHeader>

  <div class="dyn-page wrap-wide">
    <DynamicsEngine />

    <div class="dyn-page__note prose wrap">
      <p>
        Start with gravity <strong>isolated</strong>. The cluster just sits there — it is a
        drawing. Switch to <strong>N-body</strong> and press run: the two curves in the
        right-hand panel start together at 1 and then part, the heaviest stars contracting
        relative to where they began while the lightest hold or expand. That gap <em>is</em>
        mass segregation, and it is a measurement rather than an impression — the same stars
        are followed all run, so the curves cannot separate by relabelling who counts as heavy.
        Each curve is drawn relative to its own starting radius, because the heaviest 10% is a
        small enough group that its absolute radius is a noisy number; the two absolute radii
        are in the readout, and at the start they differ by sampling alone.
      </p>
      <p>
        Watch the energy strip along the bottom while you do it. A symplectic integrator's
        error stays <em>bounded</em> — it wobbles and does not grow — which is what earns the
        right to believe the panel above it. If that trace ever walks steadily off the strip,
        the cluster you are watching is a numerical artefact.
      </p>
      <p>
        Drop <strong>N</strong> and the curves get noisier: with few stars, segregation is a
        statement about an average that barely has enough members to be an average. This is
        the same sampling problem the <a href="/explore/census">census</a> shows at the
        high-mass end, arriving from the other direction.
      </p>
      <p class="dyn-page__limits">
        <strong>What this model does and does not know.</strong> The integrator is a symplectic
        scheme of the same family my <code>gravax</code> code uses, run here on a few hundred
        stars so it keeps up with a browser; gravity is softened at small separations, so very
        close encounters are smoothed rather than resolved. The stars <em>move</em> but they do
        not <em>age</em> — masses, temperatures and luminosities are ZAMS values held fixed for
        the run, so nothing here evolves off the main sequence. And the segregation you are
        watching is earned; for the version that is imposed at birth instead, see
        <a href="/explore/mass-segregation">primordial mass segregation</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .dyn-page {
    padding-block: var(--space-l) var(--space-3xl);
  }
  .dyn-page__note {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-2xl);
  }
  .dyn-page__limits {
    padding-block-start: var(--space-s);
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--border);
    color: var(--ink-dim);
  }
</style>
